# The INPUT of requirements-ci.txt, the CI test environment
# (.github/workflows/ci.yml). Edit this file, never the generated one.
#
#   python3 scripts/compile_locks.py            regenerate every hashed lock
#   python3 scripts/compile_locks.py --check    what CI runs
#
# This mirrors the environment the known-failures baseline
# (tests/known_failures.txt) was generated in, so the baseline-diff gate is
# reproducible.
#
# IT MUST COVER EVERY MODULE requirements.lock PINS. That is enforced by
# tests/test_ci_env_matches_lock.py, and it is why the first line below
# includes the lock's own input instead of repeating it.
#
# It used to end: "Optional, heavy, or import-guarded extras (anthropic,
# mplfinance/matplotlib/pandas) are intentionally omitted — their absence is
# part of the baseline." Every clause was true and the conclusion was backwards.
# `requirements.lock` says a module is GUARANTEED PRESENT in production; a CI
# environment that omits it tests a system nobody runs, and the omission is
# invisible because the tests that need it skip. Twenty chart tests skipped here
# on every green run for months while /chart quietly sent text.
#
# "Import-guarded" was the specific trap. Pillow is guarded at eleven sites and
# is not optional at all — a guarded import means the code SURVIVES the absence,
# not that the feature is expendable. Being heavy is a reason to cache the
# install, not to stop testing the feature.
#
# Every line is an exact pin, the transitives included, for the reason
# requirements.in gives. Until 2026-11-19 the generated file carried no
# hashes: it named 29 packages and pip installs about a hundred for them, and
# pip refuses a hashed file that leaves one out.
-r requirements.in

